# GH-256: `forjar dist` output. Release builds it into /tmp/dist-output; a copy
# committed at the repo root in March went stale against the maintained
# install.sh and carried 7 bashrs SEC findings the real one does not.
/dist-output/
.dogfood/
.pv/

# pmat writes a `.pmat/` cache wherever it is INVOKED, not just at the repo
# root. A run from src/verb/ or tests/ leaves a nested one holding tdg-*.db
# scratch; on 2026-08-23 a `git add -A src/` staged eight of them.
#
# Unanchored so it catches every depth, which also covers the root — that is
# deliberate. The root `.pmat/` files the ratchets read (baseline.json,
# cb200-baseline.json, context.db) are already TRACKED, and gitignore never
# affects a tracked file, so they keep working. What this stops is a NEW cache
# artefact drifting into a commit from either place. Adding a genuinely new
# tracked file under .pmat/ needs `git add -f`, which is the right amount of
# friction for a directory that is otherwise all scratch.
**/.pmat/
